#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# qr

#% text
# `qr(m)` gives `[q, r]` with `m == dot(q, r)`, `q` with orthonormal columns and `r` upper triangular.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Machine matrices use Householder reflections; exact matrices give exact factors from Gram-Schmidt, with square roots where the column lengths need them.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
qr([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])
#% output
#= [[[0.3162277660168378, 0.9486832980505135], [0.9486832980505135, -0.3162277660168381]], [[3.162277660168378, 4.427188724235729], [0.0, 0.6324555320336742]]]

#% cell
qr([[3, 0], [4, 5]])
#% output
#= [[[3/5, -4/5], [4/5, 3/5]], [[5, 4], [0, 3]]]

#% cell
qr([[1.0, 0.0], [1.0, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0]])
#% output
#= [[[0.7071067811865475, -0.4082482904638627], [0.7071067811865476, 0.4082482904638627], [-0.0, 0.8164965809277259]], [[1.414213562373095, 0.7071067811865476], [0.0, 1.2247448713915885]]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The factors give the matrix back and `q` is orthogonal:

#% cell
m = [[2.0, 1.0], [1.0, 3.0], [0.0, 1.0]]; f = qr(m); [norm(flatten(dot(f[1], f[2]) - m)) < 1.0e-14, round(dot(transpose(f[1]), f[1]), 1.0e-12)]
#% output
#= [true, [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]]]

